From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: explorer: the session's Linux programs' windows on the taskbar

A Linux program's window -- the Linux terminal -- is an X top-level of its
own beside Wine's desktop window: no shell hook reports it, and it had no
taskbar button (QA: the terminal "does not show up" there).

The compositor lists them (sg-compositor sg13: XWINDOWS on its control
socket -- window id, shown or minimized, focused, class, title); the bar
asks once a second through sg-lockctl, which writes the list to a file in
the session's runtime directory (XWINDOWS --out: Wine hands a native program
no pipe for its output), read at the next tick. Each window gets a button
with its title (and a terminal's icon for a terminal), behind a hidden
stand-in window that holds the title. A click brings it forward
(XACTIVATE), or minimizes it when it is in front (XMINIMIZE); its menu's
Close window closes it (XCLOSE). Gone from the list, its button goes.
SG_LOCKCTL may name another sg-lockctl (the gate's stand-in).

Gate: test/xwin-taskbar-gate.sh.
